gle turn. To beat the Landlord\'s pair of 3s, I would have to play my pair of 5s (or higher), which consumes both of my 5s, permanently breaking the straight. Breaking the straight would leave me with a much slower hand composed of scattered singles and pairs. Since the Landlord\'s played hand is the lowest possible pair, my teammate likely has a pair to answer, or I can find a better opportunity later. Preserving the straight is the optimal long-term strategy.\n\nReason: Playing a pair of 5s would break the 12-card straight (3-A), which is the most efficient way to clear my hand (reducing required turns from roughly 7 to 6). Passing allows me to keep this win condition intact while letting my teammate handle the weak opening play.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
